# Result

The Register File successfully stored and retrieved data using register addresses.

Two registers could be read simultaneously, while data could be written to a selected register on the clock edge.

The simulation confirmed that the registers behaved independently and that writes were only performed when "Write Enable" was active.

---

# Reflection

The Register File was an important step toward understanding how a CPU is constructed.

ROM and RAM demonstrated general memory concepts, while the Register File showed how a CPU can have a small collection of fast-access storage locations specifically designed for instruction operands.

The two read ports were particularly important because they demonstrated how a CPU can obtain two operands at the same time before sending them to an ALU.

This project connected the memory concepts from the previous projects to the architecture of a CPU.


---

# Problems

The main challenge was understanding the difference between a general RAM and a Register File.

The Register File uses multiple read ports so that several registers can be accessed at the same time.

Another challenge was ensuring that writes only occurred on the correct clock edge and when "Write Enable" was active.

Simulation was used to verify the read and write behavior.

